Every datagram a host sends goes through one place, and the program can put a hook there.

Our own patch (AvP), for protocol version 2 (CRYPTO.md section 9, in the AVPx-Dedicated-Server
repository): through a relay, every datagram the game's ENet sends between a joiner and its host
is sealed end to end, so the relay can't read or change it. ENet lets a program see every packet
that comes in (ENetHost::intercept), but has nothing for the ones that go out: it calls the
socket itself. Its compressor can't stand in, because it leaves ENet's header as it was, and is
used only when its output is smaller.

- A new ENetHost::sendHook (ENetSendCallback). When the program sets it, every datagram the host
  sends goes to the hook instead of the socket, and the hook sends it (or doesn't). It answers as
  enet_socket_send does; a datagram it drops on purpose it counts as sent, and ENet takes it as
  one lost on the way, as it does any lost datagram.
- The host's two places that send (its connections' commands, and patch 006's answers outside a
  connection) now both call one function, enet_protocol_send_datagram, which is the only caller
  of enet_socket_send left in ENet. So nothing can leave around the hook.
- With no hook set, nothing changes: the same calls reach the socket as before.

